{0 Router}

{!Workero.Router} picks a handler from the method and the path of a request. It
adds path parameters, catch-all paths and [405] answers to a plain [match] on
{!Workero.Request.method_} and {!Workero.Request.path}.

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let router =
  Router.make
    [
      Router.get "/" (fun _ _ _ _ -> Response.text "home\n");
      Router.get "/users/:id<int>" (fun params _ _ _ ->
          Response.text ("user " ^ Router.param_exn params "id" ^ "\n"));
      Router.get "/files/:path<string...>" (fun params _ _ _ ->
          Response.text ("file " ^ Router.param_exn params "path" ^ "\n"));
      Router.post "/echo" (fun _ req _ _ -> Response.text (Request.text req));
    ]

let () = Workero.serve (Router.to_handler router)
]}

A handler takes the parameters, the request, the environment and the context.
Read a parameter with {!Workero.Router.param} or {!Workero.Router.param_exn}.

{1 Routes}

{!Workero.Router.get}, {!Workero.Router.post}, {!Workero.Router.put},
{!Workero.Router.patch}, {!Workero.Router.delete}, {!Workero.Router.head} and
{!Workero.Router.options} make a route for one method.
{!Workero.Router.any} makes a route for every method; it is tried after the
routes of the request method.

{!Workero.Router.scope} adds a prefix to a list of routes. {!Workero.Router.wrap}
applies a function on handlers to each route, for example to add a header:

{@ocaml[
let with_cors handler params req env ctx =
  let res = handler params req env ctx in
  Response.copy res
    ~headers:(("access-control-allow-origin", "*") :: Response.headers res)

let routes =
  Router.scope "/api"
    (Router.wrap with_cors [ Router.get "/ping" (fun _ _ _ _ -> Response.text "pong") ])
]}

{1 Patterns}

The pattern syntax is the one of the path patterns of
{{:https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/server-reason-react}server-reason-react}
routes. A pattern is [/], or segments that each start with [/].

{table
{tr {th Segment} {th Matches}}
{tr {td [users]} {td The decoded segment [users]. Allowed characters are
letters, digits, [- . _ ~ ! $ & ' ( ) * + , ; = : @] and bytes from 0x80, so
[café] matches [caf%C3%A9]. [.] and [..] are not allowed.}}
{tr {td [:id<string>]} {td One segment.}}
{tr {td [:id<int>]} {td One segment of plain decimal digits, with an optional
leading [-].}}
{tr {td [:ok<bool>]} {td [true] or [false].}}
{tr {td [:id<Note_id.t>]} {td One segment. A module path ending in [.t] is
accepted and matches like [string].}}
{tr {td [:rest<string...>]} {td At least one segment, up to the end of the
path. It must be the last segment. The value is the decoded segments joined with
[/].}}
}

A parameter name starts with a lower case letter or [_] and continues with
letters, digits, [_] and [']. A value that does not parse as an [int] or a
[bool] gives [400]. There are no optional segments, and a pattern has no
trailing slash.

{1 Matching}

The router splits the raw path first and decodes each segment after, so [%2F]
never splits a segment. A path with an encoded slash, a malformed escape, a dot
segment such as [%2e%2e], a control character or invalid UTF-8 gets [400]. [+]
stays [+].

When several routes match, the most specific wins: a static segment beats a
parameter, and a pattern without a catch-all beats a catch-all pattern with the
same number of static segments. {!Workero.Router.make} raises
[Invalid_argument] when a pattern is invalid, when a method and pattern appear
twice, and when two patterns of one method could match the same path with the
same specificity, such as [/:a<string>/x] and [/x/:b<string>]. The same rules
hold in server-reason-react.

{1 Answers}

{!Workero.Router.to_handler} answers these cases itself:

- No route matches: [404] [Not Found], or the handler of [~not_found].
- The path matches a route of another method: [405] with an [Allow] header. It
  is not sent for [HEAD], [OPTIONS] and [TRACE]. [HEAD] does not fall back to
  [GET].
- A bad path or parameter: [400].
- A path with trailing slashes: [308] to the path without them, with the query
  kept. [~trailing_slash:`Reject] answers [not_found] instead.

{!Workero.Router.find} does the lookup alone, without a request. Use it to
test your routes or to dispatch yourself.
